WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.950 --> 00:00:08.350
Please support our work over
at Patreon.com/ProjectCensored

00:00:10.166 --> 00:00:13.186
Thanks everyone for joining us back
at the Project Censored radio show.

00:00:13.186 --> 00:00:17.136
We're very glad right now to be joined
by John Kiriakou, who is a former

00:00:17.136 --> 00:00:20.816
CIA counterterrorism officer and a
former senior investigator with the

00:00:20.816 --> 00:00:22.386
Senate Foreign Relations Committee.

00:00:22.836 --> 00:00:26.156
John became the sixth whistleblower
indicted by the Obama administration

00:00:26.176 --> 00:00:29.786
under the Espionage Act, a
law designed to punish spies.

00:00:30.056 --> 00:00:33.936
He served 23 months in prison as
a result of his attempts to oppose

00:00:34.341 --> 00:00:37.691
and blow the whistle on the Bush
administration's torture program.

00:00:37.691 --> 00:00:39.551
John, thank you so much for joining us.

00:00:39.871 --> 00:00:41.031
Thanks for having me, Eleanor.

00:00:41.041 --> 00:00:41.731
Happy to do it.

00:00:42.571 --> 00:00:46.911
So, starting off here, I want, because
you and Assange have this in common,

00:00:46.911 --> 00:00:54.096
that you're both being pummeled by the
Espionage Act, and he would, like you,

00:00:54.136 --> 00:00:57.506
end up in the Eastern District Court
of Virginia, and I was wondering if you

00:00:57.506 --> 00:01:01.586
could give folks some context of what
that means to be in the Eastern District

00:01:01.606 --> 00:01:05.936
Court, and how that flies in the face
of the argument that were Assange to be

00:01:05.936 --> 00:01:07.826
extradited, he would receive a fair trial.

00:01:08.691 --> 00:01:09.151
Oh, yeah.

00:01:09.311 --> 00:01:11.111
First of all, bottom line up front.

00:01:11.381 --> 00:01:13.961
It's not possible for him to
receive a fair trial in the

00:01:13.961 --> 00:01:15.131
Eastern District of Virginia.

00:01:15.201 --> 00:01:16.261
It's just not possible.

00:01:16.791 --> 00:01:22.451
The easiest reason for that is that his
jury would be made up of people who work

00:01:22.451 --> 00:01:27.321
for or who have friends or relatives
who work for the CIA, the Pentagon,

00:01:27.461 --> 00:01:31.261
the Department of Homeland Security,
or any number of dozens and dozens of

00:01:31.261 --> 00:01:33.001
intelligence community contractors.

00:01:33.171 --> 00:01:34.401
That's the jury pool.

00:01:34.851 --> 00:01:38.151
So it's just not possible
to, to get a fair trial.

00:01:38.321 --> 00:01:40.121
And I'm going to ask
a rhetorical question.

00:01:40.621 --> 00:01:43.601
You know, I was charged in the
Eastern District of Virginia.

00:01:44.211 --> 00:01:47.791
Jeffrey Sterling, the CIA whistleblower,
was in the Eastern District.

00:01:49.711 --> 00:01:52.601
Snowden has been charged in the
Eastern District, as has Julian.

00:01:53.681 --> 00:01:59.031
But when former CIA Director David
Petraeus exposed the names of 10 covert

00:01:59.031 --> 00:02:02.946
CIA operatives to his girlfriend, and
gave her access to the president's

00:02:02.946 --> 00:02:07.776
black books, which were the most highly
classified documents that exist in the

00:02:07.776 --> 00:02:10.276
American government, where was he charged?

00:02:10.736 --> 00:02:14.036
He was charged in the Western
district of North Carolina.

00:02:14.806 --> 00:02:17.276
They knocked his charge
down to a misdemeanor.

00:02:17.716 --> 00:02:23.776
He took a plea and he got 18
months of unsupervised probation.

00:02:24.386 --> 00:02:26.036
He kept his security clearance.

00:02:26.536 --> 00:02:28.746
He kept his contract with the white house.

00:02:29.221 --> 00:02:33.161
And at his sentencing hearing, the
judge came down from the bench to

00:02:33.161 --> 00:02:36.531
shake his hand and to thank him
for his service to the country.

00:02:37.691 --> 00:02:42.251
So there's a big difference in the
way people are treated under the

00:02:42.271 --> 00:02:46.661
Espionage Act, depending on which
federal district you're charged in.

00:02:46.871 --> 00:02:51.541
They call the Eastern District of
Virginia the Espionage Court for a reason.

00:02:52.326 --> 00:02:56.226
And it's that no national security
defendant has ever won a case there.

00:02:56.486 --> 00:02:56.766
Ever.

00:02:59.696 --> 00:03:04.366
And I'm curious, too, because in
your trial, as I understand it, and

00:03:04.366 --> 00:03:07.766
I'm hoping you can speak more to
this, you weren't actually allowed

00:03:07.956 --> 00:03:09.556
to put up a defense for yourself.

00:03:09.656 --> 00:03:09.796
No.

00:03:10.676 --> 00:03:11.036
No.

00:03:11.256 --> 00:03:14.526
That's, that's one of the
quirks of the Espionage Act.

00:03:15.136 --> 00:03:16.766
There is no affirmative defense.

00:03:17.096 --> 00:03:23.481
You can't say, yes, I blew the whistle
on the CIA's torture program, but I did

00:03:23.481 --> 00:03:25.531
it because it was an illegal program.

00:03:26.561 --> 00:03:32.711
All you can say is, yes, I
exposed classified information.

00:03:32.871 --> 00:03:36.481
You can't, you're forbidden
from saying why you did it.

00:03:38.031 --> 00:03:41.281
And Ed Snowden, I was in close
touch with Ed Snowden after

00:03:41.281 --> 00:03:42.831
he first made his revelations.

00:03:43.741 --> 00:03:49.031
Well, first of all, he said he was
willing to come home and face the music.

00:03:49.361 --> 00:03:53.221
And I said, listen, you've got to hire
the best lawyers that money can buy.

00:03:53.391 --> 00:03:55.831
You should hire my lawyers, right?

00:03:56.011 --> 00:04:01.521
And so he did, he hired my lawyers and
they immediately engaged in conversations

00:04:01.521 --> 00:04:06.431
with the justice department to try to
work out a deal because as I said, he was

00:04:06.441 --> 00:04:11.961
willing to come home and face the music
and go to prison, he told me this himself.

00:04:12.771 --> 00:04:18.471
If they would allow him to stand up in
court to explain why he did what he did.

00:04:19.161 --> 00:04:22.141
And they said, absolutely not.

00:04:23.011 --> 00:04:28.391
So for whatever reason, that's never
been explained, it was better for the

00:04:28.391 --> 00:04:33.271
justice department for Ed Snowden to make
a new life for himself in Russia than

00:04:33.281 --> 00:04:40.231
it was for him to come back and explain
that the CIA and NSA and myriad other

00:04:40.231 --> 00:04:43.281
services are spying on American citizens.

00:04:43.851 --> 00:04:44.521
They wouldn't do it.

00:04:46.091 --> 00:04:48.881
And it's really, I mean, if
you think about it, intention

00:04:48.881 --> 00:04:50.981
is really a big deal in court.

00:04:50.981 --> 00:04:53.121
Because it separates, of
course, murder trials.

00:04:53.121 --> 00:04:56.231
Like, did you mean to kill the person,
or did you accidentally kill them?

00:04:56.231 --> 00:05:00.221
So intention is really a vital
point of the justice system.

00:05:00.461 --> 00:05:02.611
But not in the Espionage Act.

00:05:04.691 --> 00:05:09.066
My lawyers actually tried to make that
argument in the very first hearing

00:05:09.106 --> 00:05:15.856
that we had and, my judge, Judge Leonie
Brinkman, a Reagan appointee, she

00:05:15.856 --> 00:05:20.976
interrupted the lawyers and she said,
I'm not going to respect precedent

00:05:21.346 --> 00:05:26.766
from other courts  that the defendant
had to have a criminal intent.

00:05:28.126 --> 00:05:31.756
And my lawyers are saying, well, wait a
minute, your honor, are you saying that a

00:05:31.756 --> 00:05:34.936
person can accidentally commit espionage?

00:05:35.811 --> 00:05:38.271
And she said, that's
exactly what I'm saying.

00:05:38.301 --> 00:05:40.811
And then she turned to
me and she said, Mr.

00:05:40.811 --> 00:05:43.961
Kiriakou, you either did
it or you didn't do it.

00:05:44.181 --> 00:05:45.311
And I think you did it.

00:05:46.761 --> 00:05:47.361
And that was it.

00:05:48.151 --> 00:05:50.661
We blocked off three days.

00:05:51.651 --> 00:05:58.131
We wrote up hundreds of
motions to throw out documents.

00:05:58.761 --> 00:06:02.861
And we blocked off three days for
her to hear these 200 motions.

00:06:03.131 --> 00:06:04.181
So we walked in.

00:06:04.546 --> 00:06:07.046
And they were all about
criminal intent, right?

00:06:07.046 --> 00:06:13.131
Because they showed, these cables
showed my refusal to take part in

00:06:13.131 --> 00:06:17.601
the torture training, my objection
to torture while I was still in the

00:06:17.601 --> 00:06:25.861
CIA, and there were dozens, hundreds
of cables that laid out the actual

00:06:25.861 --> 00:06:27.641
torture techniques that were being used.

00:06:28.191 --> 00:06:31.791
And she said, we walked into the
courtroom, and she said, I'm going

00:06:31.791 --> 00:06:36.721
to save everybody a lot of time, and
I'm denying all 200 of these motions.

00:06:38.221 --> 00:06:40.021
And so, that was it.

00:06:40.491 --> 00:06:45.681
She declared us in recess and as
we were walking out, I said to my

00:06:45.681 --> 00:06:47.861
lead attorney, what just happened?

00:06:48.581 --> 00:06:50.301
And he said, we just lost the case.

00:06:50.331 --> 00:06:51.101
That's what happened.

00:06:52.341 --> 00:06:54.391
And that's the Eastern
District of Virginia.

00:06:54.621 --> 00:07:00.951
In the end I got a best and final
offer from the Justice Department

00:07:01.021 --> 00:07:03.021
and I decided to turn it down.

00:07:03.111 --> 00:07:09.606
My wife and I stayed up all night talking
about what to do and I believed in my

00:07:09.606 --> 00:07:14.396
heart that I was innocent and I was going
to turn it down and I said very naively,

00:07:14.876 --> 00:07:19.556
once I get in front of a jury and I
explain what happened, they're going to

00:07:19.556 --> 00:07:21.616
see how ridiculous these charges are.

00:07:22.156 --> 00:07:26.476
So I emailed my attorneys very early
in the morning and they responded

00:07:26.476 --> 00:07:29.886
immediately that three of them were
coming over to the house, four of

00:07:29.886 --> 00:07:31.206
them were coming over to the house.

00:07:31.786 --> 00:07:35.796
The one who was the oldest and the
most grizzled when he walked in, he

00:07:35.796 --> 00:07:37.446
said to me, these were his exact words.

00:07:38.346 --> 00:07:41.566
He said, you stupid son
of a bitch, take the deal.

00:07:43.016 --> 00:07:48.196
And then the second one who was kind
of this Southern gentleman, he said,

00:07:48.196 --> 00:07:53.206
listen, if you were my own brother,
I would beg you to take this deal.

00:07:53.696 --> 00:07:55.836
It's not going to get
any better than this.

00:07:57.486 --> 00:08:02.346
And then the third one who was
tough but who I liked and respected

00:08:02.346 --> 00:08:05.246
the most got right in my face.

00:08:05.806 --> 00:08:09.646
And forgive me if I've told this story too
many times, but got right in my face and

00:08:09.646 --> 00:08:13.646
he said, you know what your problem is,
your problem is you think this is about

00:08:13.656 --> 00:08:19.186
justice and it's not about justice, it's
about mitigating damage, take the deal.

00:08:20.666 --> 00:08:21.776
And so I took the deal.

00:08:23.786 --> 00:08:25.076
And that's what they expect.

00:08:25.346 --> 00:08:30.906
That's why, according to ProPublica,
the federal government wins 98.

00:08:30.956 --> 00:08:37.746
2 percent of its cases and in the Eastern
District of Virginia, they win 99.

00:08:37.796 --> 00:08:39.546
1 percent of their cases.

00:08:40.371 --> 00:08:41.391
You don't have a chance.

00:08:41.801 --> 00:08:42.721
You can't win.

00:08:44.551 --> 00:08:48.691
And as you've said before, Julian
Assange would not, I mean, there's no

00:08:48.691 --> 00:08:53.041
way that he would be offered a deal by
the Justice Department at this point.

00:08:54.601 --> 00:08:55.251
I doubt it.

00:08:55.891 --> 00:09:00.311
The only way I could see the Justice
Department offering him a deal would

00:09:00.321 --> 00:09:05.611
be if he or if Wikileaks had additional
information that had not yet been

00:09:05.871 --> 00:09:10.556
released and as part of the deal,
they would negotiate, you know, X

00:09:10.556 --> 00:09:14.816
amount of time in exchange for you
not releasing the information or

00:09:14.816 --> 00:09:16.726
returning it back to the government.

00:09:17.516 --> 00:09:25.366
But no, he's facing 175 years in
prison on dozens of espionage charges.

00:09:26.116 --> 00:09:30.096
I can't imagine that they would want
to make that any easier for him.

00:09:31.996 --> 00:09:36.326
And in your experience too, it's
not just about the Eastern District,

00:09:36.366 --> 00:09:40.576
it's also about trying to ensure that
lives are destroyed after the fact.

00:09:41.016 --> 00:09:44.156
And I was wondering if you could
share a little bit about that.

00:09:44.156 --> 00:09:47.356
So  let's say that
somehow he was pardoned.

00:09:47.526 --> 00:09:51.176
There's also this large state
apparatus that tries to ensure that

00:09:51.176 --> 00:09:54.736
whistleblowers and truth tellers
have their lives destroyed even

00:09:54.736 --> 00:09:56.396
outside of the justice system.

00:09:57.381 --> 00:10:01.451
Yeah, you know, automatically
people walk away from you, right?

00:10:01.491 --> 00:10:07.491
Friends, former colleagues, even
relatives will just walk away from you

00:10:07.971 --> 00:10:09.721
and they'll never speak to you again.

00:10:09.921 --> 00:10:13.286
That's hurtful, right?

00:10:13.376 --> 00:10:14.636
But you can live with that.

00:10:15.026 --> 00:10:17.926
Okay, you get to see who your
friends really were anyway.

00:10:18.046 --> 00:10:20.106
And then you make new
friends in a new community.

00:10:20.116 --> 00:10:21.546
So you're okay there.

00:10:22.176 --> 00:10:27.656
But where you're not okay is you will
never work in your field ever again.

00:10:28.686 --> 00:10:32.396
And on top of never working in
your field, you have a national

00:10:32.396 --> 00:10:34.236
security felony conviction.

00:10:35.296 --> 00:10:36.936
So you lose your federal pension.

00:10:37.766 --> 00:10:41.606
You lose the right to vote.

00:10:42.126 --> 00:10:44.416
You lose the right to ever own a firearm.

00:10:45.306 --> 00:10:50.246
You're always a suspect in something.

00:10:50.546 --> 00:10:53.796
I mean, it was years after I got
home from prison that the FBI

00:10:53.846 --> 00:10:55.296
continued to follow me around.

00:10:55.356 --> 00:10:58.166
Not all the time, but
with some regularity.

00:10:59.006 --> 00:11:04.696
And so, here, myself as an
example, I was one of the U.S.

00:11:04.696 --> 00:11:06.826
government's leading
experts on the Middle East.

00:11:07.926 --> 00:11:12.436
And I ended up stocking shelves at
Michael's craft store on midnight

00:11:12.436 --> 00:11:17.806
shift before I got a minimum wage
job at a left wing think tank,

00:11:18.626 --> 00:11:22.051
and then finally my wife left.

00:11:22.601 --> 00:11:23.631
She couldn't take it anymore.

00:11:24.651 --> 00:11:27.751
So, that's all part of the
longer term punishment.

00:11:28.141 --> 00:11:29.431
They want you to be ruined.

00:11:29.451 --> 00:11:33.621
Not because they specifically
have it out for you, right?

00:11:34.341 --> 00:11:38.221
They're not sitting around a table
saying, how can we screw Eleanor?

00:11:38.221 --> 00:11:40.701
How can we make it so
she never works again?

00:11:41.401 --> 00:11:45.851
What they're doing though is saying,
how can we use Eleanor as an example?

00:11:46.301 --> 00:11:52.146
Where we make her so hurt that
other people are going to look

00:11:52.146 --> 00:11:55.436
at her and say, you know, I was
thinking about blowing the whistle,

00:11:55.436 --> 00:11:56.646
but look what they did to Eleanor.

00:11:57.306 --> 00:11:58.536
I better keep my mouth shut.

00:11:59.836 --> 00:12:04.286
A New York Times reporter told me that
on the day of my arrest, literally

00:12:04.326 --> 00:12:08.166
every one of the New York Times
national security sources went silent

00:12:08.566 --> 00:12:10.606
and stayed silent for six months.

00:12:12.476 --> 00:12:13.416
And that was the goal.

00:12:13.736 --> 00:12:14.886
That's what they wanted to do.

00:12:16.416 --> 00:12:19.536
Wow, that is terrifying.

00:12:20.106 --> 00:12:25.426
Well, and I'm curious too, because
you worked at the CIA, and because

00:12:25.556 --> 00:12:30.526
the CIA has been particularly rabid
against Julian Assange, were you

00:12:30.526 --> 00:12:34.646
surprised when you heard, for instance,
that Pompeo had hatched this plan

00:12:34.646 --> 00:12:36.236
to have Julian Assange murdered?

00:12:36.246 --> 00:12:41.686
Did that seem in step and in line with
how the CIA operates on the inside?

00:12:42.466 --> 00:12:43.026
Yes.

00:12:43.736 --> 00:12:45.586
And I'll tell you why.

00:12:45.696 --> 00:12:47.996
I was sickened, of course, by this report.

00:12:47.996 --> 00:12:50.116
You're talking about the
report from Yahoo News.

00:12:50.516 --> 00:12:52.876
I was sickened by it,
but not at all surprised.

00:12:53.566 --> 00:12:57.516
And there are a couple of little details
in there that are not generally publicly

00:12:57.516 --> 00:12:58.996
known that I think are very important.

00:12:59.056 --> 00:13:05.016
So what you're talking about is a report
by Michael Isikoff that ran in Yahoo News.

00:13:05.016 --> 00:13:10.336
Now, Mike Isikoff is a very well
known, very highly respected national

00:13:10.336 --> 00:13:11.716
security journalist here in Washington.

00:13:11.716 --> 00:13:16.821
He's a Pulitzer Prize winner, made
his made his bones at Newsweek when

00:13:16.831 --> 00:13:20.491
Newsweek was a major publication.

00:13:21.071 --> 00:13:25.351
He was able to get 30 current
and former intelligence officers

00:13:25.351 --> 00:13:28.281
to talk to him for this report.

00:13:28.731 --> 00:13:32.291
So it's not like just one guy said, Oh,
Pompeo wanted to kill Julian Assange.

00:13:32.571 --> 00:13:37.041
This is 30 people from the inside who
gave the details of this operation.

00:13:37.041 --> 00:13:45.821
Now the idea was if Julian attempted
to leave the Ecuadorian embassy that

00:13:45.831 --> 00:13:52.246
he would be snatched off the street
and rendered, either to the Eastern

00:13:52.246 --> 00:13:56.186
District of Virginia to face trial,
or to Guantanamo to be held until they

00:13:56.186 --> 00:13:57.276
could figure out what to do with him.

00:13:58.086 --> 00:14:02.506
Or, if they couldn't render him,
to shoot him dead in the street.

00:14:03.396 --> 00:14:09.526
They also talked about a Plan C: that if
he were somehow to make his way to one

00:14:09.526 --> 00:14:17.246
of London's airports and board a Russian
embassy flight, the CIA was authorized

00:14:17.256 --> 00:14:19.766
to shoot out the tires of the plane.

00:14:19.926 --> 00:14:24.146
Now this is an act of war, to
shoot out the tires of the plane to

00:14:24.146 --> 00:14:25.606
ensure that it couldn't take off.

00:14:26.436 --> 00:14:34.276
Okay, one of the things that most
people missed was a couple of days

00:14:34.306 --> 00:14:40.236
before this became public, Mike Pompeo,
in an interview, called WikiLeaks a

00:14:40.236 --> 00:14:43.356
hostile, non state intelligence service.

00:14:44.381 --> 00:14:50.231
Those words were very carefully
chosen, because if WikiLeaks is a

00:14:50.231 --> 00:14:55.871
hostile, non state, intelligence
service, that makes this whole

00:14:55.891 --> 00:14:58.571
case a counterintelligence case.

00:14:58.971 --> 00:15:04.176
Now, a counterintelligence
case would be run by the CIA's

00:15:04.176 --> 00:15:05.271
counterintelligence center.

00:15:06.101 --> 00:15:10.481
But counterintelligence cases
are the most highly classified

00:15:10.481 --> 00:15:14.171
cases that the CIA handles.

00:15:14.911 --> 00:15:20.391
They're so highly classified that
they are the only cases that don't

00:15:20.401 --> 00:15:23.861
have to be briefed to the House
and Senate oversight committees.

00:15:24.241 --> 00:15:24.741
Why?

00:15:25.501 --> 00:15:32.411
Because counterintelligence usually
means that you're working for a

00:15:32.411 --> 00:15:34.741
foreign power, a foreign government.

00:15:35.251 --> 00:15:39.991
Well, if the CIA is investigating
a mole, who's to say that the mole

00:15:40.011 --> 00:15:44.856
maybe isn't the chairman of the
Senate Intelligence Committee, right?

00:15:44.906 --> 00:15:47.656
Or the ranking member of the
House Intelligence Committee.

00:15:47.976 --> 00:15:50.656
So those are all held internally.

00:15:51.666 --> 00:15:54.936
Well, if this operation were to be
carried out, all anybody would know

00:15:54.946 --> 00:15:58.706
was that Julian Assange tried to leave
the embassy, the Ecuadorian embassy in

00:15:58.706 --> 00:16:00.766
London, and he was shot dead, period.

00:16:01.236 --> 00:16:02.486
And that's the end of the story.

00:16:03.376 --> 00:16:09.296
That's why he used those very specific
words, that very specific term, or

00:16:09.296 --> 00:16:14.486
terminology, that it was a hostile
non state intelligence service.

00:16:14.956 --> 00:16:15.871
And of course it's not.

00:16:16.811 --> 00:16:21.651
It's a transparency and journalism
outlet, but that's what they

00:16:21.651 --> 00:16:23.071
don't want people to think.

00:16:24.231 --> 00:16:29.341
And let me add one thing, and
this is just an educated guess.

00:16:29.341 --> 00:16:33.651
I don't have any inside information to
prove that this is the case, but I think

00:16:33.681 --> 00:16:44.701
the reason why this never happened was
the modus operandi for a covert action

00:16:45.101 --> 00:16:49.811
program like this is you first go to
the CIA's general counsel, they say,

00:16:50.001 --> 00:16:52.061
yes, it's legal, no, it's not legal.

00:16:52.191 --> 00:16:55.881
If it's legal, it goes to the Justice
Department's Office of Legal Counsel,

00:16:55.941 --> 00:16:58.681
OLC, and they say, yes, it's legal.

00:16:58.701 --> 00:17:00.751
No, it's not legal.

00:17:00.921 --> 00:17:08.321
If they say, then it goes to the National
Security Council General Counsel and

00:17:08.321 --> 00:17:10.231
he'll say yes, it's legal, no, it's not.

00:17:10.961 --> 00:17:14.941
If it is, it goes to the national
security advisor for a signature.

00:17:15.531 --> 00:17:18.881
If that person signs it, it goes
to the president for his signature.

00:17:19.191 --> 00:17:21.351
And if the president signs
it, it's implemented.

00:17:21.991 --> 00:17:25.731
I think that it made its way to the N.S.C.

00:17:26.331 --> 00:17:30.641
And I think the national security
advisor received it and said,

00:17:31.291 --> 00:17:33.791
are they out of their minds?

00:17:34.571 --> 00:17:40.471
We are going to assassinate a
Five Eyes citizen, a citizen of

00:17:40.471 --> 00:17:44.781
Australia, who has never faced
his accusers in a court of law?

00:17:45.681 --> 00:17:51.291
We're gonna murder him in broad daylight
in the street in Knightsbridge, London?

00:17:52.421 --> 00:17:57.561
So somebody, probably the National
Security Advisor, was the adult

00:17:57.561 --> 00:17:59.451
in the room and killed it.

00:17:59.871 --> 00:18:04.211
But I think at the same time, there
were enough people in the CIA who

00:18:04.211 --> 00:18:10.321
were aware of the planning for it
that they said, this is above and

00:18:10.321 --> 00:18:15.951
beyond, we've got to say something
because people have lost their minds.

00:18:16.171 --> 00:18:21.861
And I think that's why this team
of Yahoo reporters didn't have one

00:18:21.861 --> 00:18:24.731
source or two sources or five sources.

00:18:25.091 --> 00:18:30.461
They had 30 sources who all
confirmed each other's information.

00:18:32.091 --> 00:18:33.501
Yeah, that is remarkable.

00:18:33.521 --> 00:18:37.161
And I want to kind of talk about that
hierarchy for a second because it does

00:18:37.161 --> 00:18:40.951
seem like it's a bit askew, right?

00:18:40.951 --> 00:18:45.091
Because yes, in that case, the
CIA could be tamped down by the

00:18:45.101 --> 00:18:47.911
NSC and basically like, no, we're
not doing that, that's ridiculous.

00:18:48.151 --> 00:18:52.531
But at the same time, as Kevin Gosztola
points out in his book, there's a

00:18:52.531 --> 00:18:55.861
good chance that Assange wouldn't be
facing the charges that he's facing

00:18:56.031 --> 00:19:01.841
were it not for the CIA's rabid stance
against Assange, because the DOJ was

00:19:01.861 --> 00:19:08.191
really scrambling to charge Assange
once Pompeo made it very clear that

00:19:08.191 --> 00:19:10.401
he wanted Julian's head on a stick.

00:19:11.521 --> 00:19:16.791
It seems like the hierarchy where
the CIA operates underneath anyone,

00:19:17.441 --> 00:19:19.226
that doesn't seem to be the case.

00:19:20.816 --> 00:19:25.916
Yeah, people, I think, generally
don't realize just how authoritative

00:19:25.936 --> 00:19:30.186
the CIA is in areas of government
where they shouldn't even be a

00:19:30.186 --> 00:19:32.676
part of the conversation, right?

00:19:32.676 --> 00:19:38.896
The CIA's job, very simply, is
to recruit spies to steal secrets

00:19:39.306 --> 00:19:43.946
and to analyze those secrets, to
allow policymakers to make the best

00:19:43.956 --> 00:19:46.656
informed policy decisions, period.

00:19:46.876 --> 00:19:47.326
That's it.

00:19:48.416 --> 00:19:52.766
It shouldn't be up to the CIA to
decide who's charged with a crime,

00:19:52.996 --> 00:19:58.166
who gets prosecuted with a crime,
to create paramilitary forces, to

00:19:58.166 --> 00:20:01.846
carry out international kidnappings
or torture programs in archipelagos

00:20:02.766 --> 00:20:07.966
of secret prisons, to decide what
Congress should and shouldn't know.

00:20:08.521 --> 00:20:12.961
None of that should be up to the CIA,
but we've allowed the CIA, like we did

00:20:12.961 --> 00:20:17.386
with the FBI in the 50s to just keep
pushing that envelope to the point

00:20:17.406 --> 00:20:20.126
where it's too late to stop them.

00:20:22.526 --> 00:20:29.036
And you need a Church Committee
or a Pike Committee to finally

00:20:29.036 --> 00:20:30.276
reign them back in again.

00:20:31.406 --> 00:20:37.601
Remember, Barack Obama, as bad as
he was, especially for national

00:20:37.601 --> 00:20:41.191
security whistleblowers never
charged Julian Assange with a crime.

00:20:42.411 --> 00:20:43.731
It was Donald Trump that did it.

00:20:43.761 --> 00:20:48.591
Now, many of us, and I'll admit that I
was just as wrong as many of my friends

00:20:48.591 --> 00:20:54.491
and colleagues, many of us thought
that, well, you know, Joe Biden was a

00:20:54.496 --> 00:20:59.741
part of the Obama administration and he
knew what Obama was talking about when

00:20:59.741 --> 00:21:04.571
he said that charging Assange would
give him a New York Times problem,

00:21:05.196 --> 00:21:06.786
and we can certainly talk about that.

00:21:07.486 --> 00:21:10.546
Certainly Biden understands the
New York Times problem and he'll

00:21:10.546 --> 00:21:11.856
have to drop these charges.

00:21:11.966 --> 00:21:13.556
No, he doubled down.

00:21:14.496 --> 00:21:19.971
He doubled down and here we are, expecting
that Julian will be extradited to the

00:21:19.981 --> 00:21:25.001
Eastern District before the end of this
year and then probably sit in pre trial

00:21:25.481 --> 00:21:30.981
detention, you know, for years while the
two sides bicker about what should be

00:21:31.121 --> 00:21:32.970
admitted as evidence and what shouldn't.

00:21:35.701 --> 00:21:35.901
Yeah.

00:21:35.901 --> 00:21:39.381
And I would definitely like you to
speak to the New York Times problem.

00:21:39.381 --> 00:21:42.441
For people who don't know, could
you briefly explain what that is

00:21:42.441 --> 00:21:44.191
before we, before we sign off?

00:21:44.481 --> 00:21:48.101
Yeah, it's one of the funny
ironies of this whole situation.

00:21:48.131 --> 00:21:52.561
You know, the Washington Post, the New
York Times, the Wall Street Journal, AP,

00:21:53.221 --> 00:21:57.306
all of these big news outlets, these big
mainstream news outlets publish classified

00:21:57.306 --> 00:21:59.586
information literally every single day.

00:22:00.606 --> 00:22:06.686
Washington couldn't run if it didn't
have classified leaks every single day.

00:22:06.716 --> 00:22:10.736
And usually it's the White House or
the Pentagon that's doing the leaking.

00:22:12.016 --> 00:22:15.946
I can point to leaks that I'm positive
came from the CIA just in the last

00:22:15.946 --> 00:22:18.296
two weeks about Israel / Palestine.

00:22:19.476 --> 00:22:24.741
But when those leaks are authorized,
eh, you know, everybody's happy.

00:22:25.621 --> 00:22:30.121
When the leaks are unauthorized, then
the White House is very upset and

00:22:30.131 --> 00:22:33.881
the CIA will file something called
a Crimes Report with the FBI, and

00:22:33.881 --> 00:22:35.541
then the FBI has to investigate it.

00:22:36.920 --> 00:22:41.910
But the truth is, if you're going to
charge Julian Assange, a publisher

00:22:42.565 --> 00:22:46.245
with multiple counts of espionage,
then you're going to have to charge the

00:22:46.245 --> 00:22:49.795
Washington Post, the New York Times,
the Wall Street Journal, the L.A.

00:22:49.795 --> 00:22:53.135
Times, AP, and everybody
else who publishes classified

00:22:53.135 --> 00:22:55.545
information every single day.

00:22:56.715 --> 00:22:59.255
Well, that's a First
Amendment violation, isn't it?

00:22:59.695 --> 00:23:05.675
So we're either going to be transparent
and supporters of freedom of speech

00:23:05.675 --> 00:23:08.555
and freedom of press, etc or we're not.

00:23:09.835 --> 00:23:10.875
Because you can't be both.

00:23:14.090 --> 00:23:17.700
Yeah, there really is that much that
hangs on this case, and I'm curious, just

00:23:17.700 --> 00:23:21.380
because when you were speaking, I was
like, an authorized leak, wouldn't you

00:23:21.380 --> 00:23:26.850
just call that sharing information, but
they still call it an authorized leak?

00:23:28.060 --> 00:23:33.280
An authorized leak is like, the
CIA leaks: we got it right on Gaza.

00:23:33.750 --> 00:23:38.220
We predicted three days before that
they were going to launch this attack.

00:23:38.985 --> 00:23:43.815
And then they leak that to the Post
and then the Post says: classified

00:23:43.835 --> 00:23:46.515
CIA paper says the CIA got it right.

00:23:46.555 --> 00:23:51.325
And then the CIA says, Oh, no,
that information was classified.

00:23:52.025 --> 00:23:53.945
It makes us look really good.

00:23:54.265 --> 00:23:57.455
We probably should report it to the FBI.

00:23:58.090 --> 00:24:02.230
But they probably won't be able to
figure out who leaked it anyway.

00:24:04.010 --> 00:24:05.300
That's an authorized leak.

00:24:05.930 --> 00:24:06.510
I see.

00:24:08.000 --> 00:24:08.180
Okay.

00:24:08.370 --> 00:24:11.080
Like accidentally posting a
really good looking picture of

00:24:11.080 --> 00:24:12.360
yourself online or something.

00:24:12.680 --> 00:24:13.050
Yeah.

00:24:13.910 --> 00:24:15.170
Exactly.

00:24:16.150 --> 00:24:17.140
Exactly.

00:24:18.740 --> 00:24:19.210
Wow.

00:24:19.690 --> 00:24:20.060
Okay.

00:24:20.540 --> 00:24:23.240
John, thank you so much for giving
this really, really important

00:24:23.240 --> 00:24:27.490
context and sharing your own
story about what happened to you.

00:24:27.500 --> 00:24:28.450
Really appreciate it.

00:24:28.740 --> 00:24:30.630
Oh, thanks for the work
that you do, Eleanor.

00:24:30.630 --> 00:24:31.350
It's important.

00:24:31.510 --> 00:24:32.320
Thanks for having me.

